## Implementation decomposition

Every increment changing `src/skills/` or `shared/` rebuilds `skills/` and passes relevant checks before commit.

1. **Project contract and knowledge.** Append business framing; add byte-identical VCS, backlog, and documentation rules; update existing architecture and acceptance sources.
2. **Self-contained catalogue.** Add exact dependencies and licence; bundle helper; update `SHARED_PLAN`, tests, normative descriptions, generated skills, and `make mo-qc`.
3. **Backend-neutral contract.** Update methodology, `mo-review`, `mo-e2e`, and relevant `mo-omnigent` boundaries; add complete schemas, blocker types, batch rules, and adjudication limit.
4. **Herdr topology and startup.** Implement exact two-pane review tab, separate naming domains, schema-driven provider arguments, visible startup, and atomic goals.
5. **Lifecycle.** Implement progress waits, blocked-state handling, actor-loss recovery, and ordinary restart.
6. **Compact transport.** Add adaptive extraction, last-boundary selection, row/byte caps, scratch lifecycle, opaque wrapper, and full-turn-retrieval amendment.
7. **Acyclic flow.** Add sequential reviews, first-pass barrier, dual QC, explicit closure, terminal adjudication, visible E2E, approval routing, and invalidation.
8. **Agentic evidence and upstream triage.** Run resize, embedded-boundary, row/byte, Claude, Codex, and scratch-feature fixtures; record only observed support.

The executor may split work only when every split remains independently green and may combine adjacent items only when an invariant cannot otherwise be green.

## Decision ledger

| Decision | Status | Rationale |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Orchestrator never reads or edits tracked-file contents | adopted | Explicit user clarification and context-budget invariant |
| Herdr mechanics belong only to `mo-herdr` | adopted | Omnigent uses its native agent paradigm |
| Executor owns spec reading, feasibility, implementation, and ordinary choices | adopted | Engineering judgment stays with repository-reading actor |
| Restart creates a new ordinary run; executor discovers repository state | adopted | Explicit clarification; no recovery machinery |
| Cross-restart session adoption/registry/state restoration | rejected | Unrequested and contrary to ordinary restart |
| Executor-right plus separate two-reviewer right-split tab | adopted | Exact requested visual topology |
| Third review-anchor pane | rejected | Contradicts the required two-pane review tab and serves no actor |
| Persistent visible native TUI sessions inside one uninterrupted run | adopted | Observability and warm-session cache economics |
| `herdr agent start` as normal actor launch | adopted | Public readiness, naming, and lifecycle around the system CLI |
| Direct `pane run`, inline/headless, or SDK actor fallback | rejected | Invisible or off-contract and loses warm TUI continuity |
| Atomic `agent prompt` for ordinary messages | adopted | Enter is delivered by construction |
| Hard-code provider model and effort flags in the specification | rejected | Installed provider/version schema and fixtures are authoritative |
| Fixture-proven provider arguments and active-selection evidence | adopted | Prevents a ready actor silently using the wrong model or effort |
| Short caller goal names only skill and task path | adopted | Process rules belong in the skill |
| Adaptive 120/200/400/800/1000 private reads | adopted | Bounds context and respects Herdr’s cap |
| Exact ASCII handoff header plus provider lower boundary | adopted | Trims tool rendering without parsing Markdown |
| Last valid settled provider boundary, not first boundary-like body line | adopted | Prevents code or prose containing `❯` or `›` from truncating transport |
| Completion footer, verdict file, nonce, or model-written sentinel | rejected | Does not prove a complete provider turn and adds protocol state |
| Opaque body, no Markdown parsing | adopted | Satisfies role firewall and real-parser contract |
| Claim pre-render terminal bytes equal provider source bytes | rejected | Rendering may alter wraps and controls |
| Compact batches capped at 180 rows | adopted | Fixtureable envelope below the terminal ceiling |
| Compact batches also capped at 49,152 UTF-8 bytes | adopted | Rows alone do not bound argv or unwrapped-line size |
| Batch fields on every continuable handoff type | adopted | Continuation never depends on prose |
| Sequential A then B review with first-pass barrier | adopted | Preserves independence and prevents contention |
| Immediate relay before peer finishes | rejected | Lets executor mutate the candidate under peer review |
| Both reviewers independently run QC | adopted | Cross-vendor evidence without orchestrator log reading |
| Orchestrator executes actor-supplied or inferred gate commands | rejected | Unsafe and violates process-only role |
| Backend-observed internal TUI command exit status as required evidence | rejected | Current public Herdr surface does not provide it; dual reports are adopted |
| Stable reviewer finding IDs and explicit closure/adjudication | adopted | Mechanical routing without semantic judgment |
| At most one adjudication per candidate/finding | adopted | Guarantees termination without orchestrator comparison of evidence novelty |
| Second same-candidate rebuttal after adjudication uphold | rejected | Recreates an unbounded dispute loop |
| Agentic E2E after two passing reviews, rerun after any commit | adopted | Acyclic fail-closed flow |
| E2E actor contacts the human directly | rejected | Orchestrator is the sole human-attention boundary |
