## Acceptance and verification

### Deterministic `make mo-qc`

The authoritative non-mutating gate covers:

- byte-identical `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`;
- generated `skills/` matching a fresh build;
- pinned bundle dependencies, self-contained outputs, copied licence, no runtime SDK import, isolated launch, timeout, and no child leak;
- catalogue unknown, model missing, and launch failure as distinct outcomes;
- AST-level skill checks with `markdown-it`;
- exact process-header grammar and every state-specific semantic matrix row;
- invalid cross-field combinations, stale candidate/reviewer identity, canonical integer and ID lists, cumulative finding accounting, and 65,536-byte boundary cases as data tests;
- monotonic reviewer IDs across candidates, invalidation without closure, eight-batch and 524,288-byte cumulative limits, and exactly one batch per relay prompt;
- direct argv relay with `shell:false`, random wrapper delimiters, NUL and invalid-UTF-8 rejection, exact trailing-newline preservation, and no retry after ambiguous acceptance;
- explicit proof that V1 executor and E2E oversized handoffs fail closed rather than pretending to support absent batch fields;
- canonical glossary entries, unique ownership, and backend-neutral versus Herdr-qualified terminology;
- collision handling over the complete actor-name set;
- restrictive current-run scratch creation, controlled-path best-effort cleanup, and lost-scratch fail-closed behavior without cross-run discovery or deletion;
- backlog open-only and no closed section;
- version-control text and trailer format;
- no unrequested changelog or document requirement;
- every source-changing fixture rebuilding `skills/` in the same increment.

Deterministic tests do not claim TUI behavior, provider continuity, Enter delivery, semantic correctness of reviewer claims, or absence of tracked-file reads. Those require review or Herdr E2E.

### Agent-required Herdr E2E

An independent observer runs a scratch feature and verifies:

1. exact executor/reviewer topology, labels, vertical panes, unchanged focus, lazy E2E tab, and safe visible leftovers after induced partial-layout failure;
2. system Claude/Codex processes launched through `agent start` with accepted model arguments;
3. same actor and pane across turns and an unprompted reference to prior context;
4. one-line `/goal` and maximum-size relay arrive as intact atomic turns, with no separate Enter and no duplicate after an induced ambiguous submission;
5. the 120/200/400/800/1000 ladder, pre-prompt boundary fingerprint, exact current-turn header cut, 65,536-byte cap, and structural Claude/Codex boundaries at 180 rows in the actual half-width vertical pane and alternate screen, including tools, Unicode, soft wraps, duplicate current and stale headers, glyph-prefixed bodies, missing anchors/boundaries, repaint, and H7b resize; the 1000-row ceiling is a project-measured fixture limit recorded in `docs/phase-0-fixtures.md`, not claimed as a public Herdr API guarantee;
6. damaged or excessive output becomes `unknown` without partial pass or fallback;
7. executor and E2E use the same extraction contract and fail closed when V1 cannot fit one batch;
8. A completes before B begins, B gets no A output, both see the same clean candidate, and mutation is barred;
9. a reviewer-owned mutating check produces `checks=FAIL`, preserves the completed review, and reaches the executor with the dirty-tree fact only after no peer is active; unexplained dirty state discards only affected gate output;
10. QC and smoke run sequentially and are interpreted by reviewers;
11. adversarial review bytes remain identical inside the executor wrapper and cannot inject commands or roles;
12. NUL, invalid UTF-8, trailing newline, delimiter-like body bytes, wrapper injection, and ambiguous delivery all follow the direct-argv fail-closed contract without duplicate relay;
13. every finding is open, explicitly closed by its originating reviewer, adjudicated, or invalidated;
14. waits wake on same-SHA completion;
15. any commit invalidates reviews, QC, and E2E;
16. restart creates new sessions and relies on repository discovery;
17. live bundled Claude catalogue discovery works without ambient `node_modules`, while launches separately establish entitlement;
18. no ordinary menus or separate Enter operations occur;
19. an instrumented harness rejects intentional tracked-content retrieval after activation while allowing fixed Git metadata; a canary value never enters orchestrator output or context;
20. reviewer A scratch survives through reviewer B and confirmed delivery, then is deleted on the controlled path; lost scratch makes the round unknown, and a new run neither discovers nor deletes old scratch;
21. actual launched actor kinds satisfy reviewer diversity, including after fallback;
22. retries terminate at their event-specific bounds, the global no-progress guard fires, and retries do not reset repeatedly within one failing gate;
23. pane badges expose role, candidate, and gate without tracked content;
24. projects without a usable `develop` branch stop at the declared precondition rather than falling back;
25. adjudication uses the existing other reviewer only, happens after both independent first passes, and one repeated post-`WITHDRAW` refusal terminates as `unresolved_dispute`.

`docs/phase-0-fixtures.md` assigns stable fixture IDs H13–H37 one-to-one to the 25 cases above, while retaining H7b for resize. The exact route remains unsupported until H7b and the exact Claude and Codex 180-row fixtures pass. If fewer than two vendors pass, implementation stops, `mo-herdr` remains fail-closed, and only reproduced gaps become backlog entries or upstream issues.

### Omnigent acceptance

When Omnigent is a currently supported route, it independently proves the role firewall, candidate semantics, reviewer independence, finding accounting, invalidation, vocabulary consistency, bounded recovery, and human boundaries through native agents. It does not assert Herdr layout or reuse Herdr extraction. If its native full-turn route is not currently supported, acceptance records that fact and does not manufacture a failing Herdr-style fixture for it.

## Implementation decomposition

Every increment changing `src/skills/` or `shared/` also runs `make skills`, includes regenerated `skills/`, and passes relevant checks before commit.

1. **Capability proof before route work.** Add the H13–H37 fixture definitions, run H7b and exact Claude/Codex 180-row half-width fixtures against the installed Herdr, and record public-surface evidence. If fewer than two vendors pass, stop route implementation and record only reproduced backlog/issues. Existing behavior is not claimed compliant, and no invisible fallback is added.
2. **Project contract and knowledge.** Append verbatim business clarifications; add byte-identical version-control, backlog, glossary, and documentation rules; reconcile canonical vocabulary in `docs/glossary.md`; update existing architecture and acceptance files and deterministic tests.
3. **Self-contained catalogue.** Add pinned build dependencies and transitive licence/notice set; bundle the shared helper for Node 22; update `SHARED_PLAN`, isolated tests, helper-description locations, generated skills, and pass `make mo-qc`.
4. **Backend-neutral role and handoff contract.** Update methodology, glossary terminology, `mo-review`, `mo-e2e`, and relevant `mo-omnigent` boundaries; add candidate/reviewer fields, exact state matrices, finding closure, adjudication, E2E semantics, invalid-combination fixtures, generated skills, and `make mo-qc`.
5. **Herdr topology, launch, and lifecycle.** Implement tabs/panes, collision-safe names, startup arguments, atomic goals, non-submitting waits, pane badges, bounded retry/no-progress counters, restart semantics, safe partial-layout behavior, generated skill, and `make mo-qc`.
6. **Compact TUI transport.** Add current-turn-aware adaptive extraction, current-run scratch ownership, row/batch/byte bounds, direct-argv opaque relay, fail-closed delivery ambiguity, and the full-turn-retrieval amendment; rebuild and pass `make mo-qc`.
7. **Acyclic feature flow.** Add sequential review, first-pass barrier, mutating-check handling, dual QC/smoke, UNKNOWN recovery, originating-reviewer closure, existing-peer adjudication, E2E, invalidation, and traceability tests; rebuild and pass `make mo-qc`.
8. **Agentic acceptance and cutover.** Run the complete scratch feature against the implemented route. Only after all route fixtures pass, remove the old inline/headless review path and record that intentional architecture change. Record only observed passes and reproduced open failures; create issues only for reproduced gaps.

The executor may split an item when every split remains independently green or combine adjacent items when an invariant cannot be green in isolation. Generated output or newly false knowledge, including glossary definitions, may not be deferred.

